messages:
ashley (acct-exec): Urgent POC blocker from Oakley (deal). Their client intermittently fails schema validation when using function-calling + json_mode. They see double-escaped arguments in the 'arguments' field sometimes and other times a trailing blob after the JSON. Can we swarm?

sam (sales-engineer): Oof. Do we have a raw HTTP example and whether they use streaming or retries?

ashley (acct-exec): They pasted this exact raw response (non-streaming):
```
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "choices": [ { "message": { "name": "create_item", "arguments": "{\\\"item_id\\\":\"abc123\\\",\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"tags\\\":[\\\"x\\\",\\\"y\\\"]}}" } } ]
}

--extra-bytes--
```
They say model=redwood-medium-v1, streaming=false, keep-alive=true, and they aggressively retry on 429/5xx. Also reported one 429 with X-Request-Id: req_deal_77.

tyler (ml-eng): Two separate issues visible: 1) double-escaped JSON in arguments (looks like we may be serializing the arguments string twice for function-calling when using certain serializers), and 2) trailing blob (TCP boundary / connection reuse). Need request ids and the raw request they sent to reproduce serializer path.

prashant (infra): Ops-bot, pull traces for req_deal_77 and adjacent request ids for that customer's IP. If worker shows stream-terminate warnings we can tie to boundary bug. Also quick mitigation: recommend they disable aggressive client retries and use our SDK with idempotency until we fix.

ops-bot: Trace found: req_deal_77 -> worker-14 (gpu-a100-2). Related ids req_deal_78. Worker log at 2026-03-24T10:31:12Z contains WARN: "stream-terminate: leftover-bytes".

tyler (ml-eng): That WARN matches the trailing-bits issue. For the double-escaped arguments, ask if they use any middle proxy or are sending JSON-encoded strings into our API body (sometimes frameworks JSON.stringify twice). Could be client-side. Can we get the client's raw HTTP request?

ashley (acct-exec): I'll request the exact curl/send snippet. Meanwhile they asked: if we recommend removing client retries, how will this affect their POC timelines and cost?

prashant (infra): Short answer for customer: disabling aggressive retries reduces duplicate token costs but can increase latency during transient failures. For a POC with a 12% retry rate you might see up to ~12% lower token spend. We can also enable SDK idempotency to avoid duplicate billing without sacrificing automatic retries.

sam (sales-engineer): They also put a note in the security questionnaire: want prompts & outputs disabled after 14 days during POC. Is that possible on Hosted?

compliance-bot: Hosted POC retention is generally 30 days by default. We can request a reduced retention under an enterprise POC agreement. For Dedicated you can set retention and disable prompt logging. I'll assign an owner to follow up.

tyler (ml-eng): Short-term engineering triage plan: 1) Verify whether our function-calling path is double-serializing arguments for certain encoders (1-2h to reproduce locally), 2) add a shim in the HTTP worker to append a small canonical delimiter when json_mode=true so concatenation is detectable (1-3h patch + unit tests), 3) run staging with a keep-alive + retry integration test replicating the customer's behavior, then 10% canary.

prashant (infra): ETA wise: serializer repro + fix if server-side ~half day; shim hotfix to add delimiter + tests ~2-4 hours; staging canary same day if low risk. We'll prioritize this since it's blocking the deal.

ashley (acct-exec): Can we give them an immediate sanitizer/guardband they can drop into Node client to avoid parse crashes while we fix server side?

sam (sales-engineer): Yes please paste something tiny and cautious.

prashant (infra): Triage Node snippet (temporary):
```js
// naive sanitizer to take the first top-level JSON object
function extractFirstJSON(s) {
  // look for matching top-level braces by scanning
  let depth = 0;
  let start = -1;
  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
    const c = s[i];
    if (c === '{') {
      if (start === -1) start = i;
      depth++;
    } else if (c === '}') {
      depth--;
      if (depth === 0 && start !== -1) return s.slice(start, i + 1);
    }
  }
  return s; // fallback
}

const safeParse = (respText) => JSON.parse(extractFirstJSON(respText));
```
Note: not production-grade, just for triage. Also advise them to enable SDK-level de-dup.

tyler (ml-eng): If the double-escape repros locally and it's server-side we'll roll a patch to the function-calling serializer to avoid double JSON.stringify. If it's client-side we'll show the customer how to stop double-encoding. Either way we'll add a unit test to catch regressions.

sam (sales-engineer): Customer asked for ETA to have something they can test end-to-end (no sanitizer). What to tell them?

prashant (infra): Tell them: we can provide a triage sanitizer now, a shim hotfix in staging within hours, and a stable fix depending on repro (server or client) within the same day for staging and next-day production canary. For serializer fix if server-side plan ~same-day to next-day depending on complexity.

ashley (acct-exec): I'll tell the customer: 1) temporary sanitizer we can paste now, 2) hotfix shim to make concat detectable in staging today, 3) serializer investigation in parallel, 4) pricing note and compliance follow-up on retention. Scheduling a 30-min call at 3pm PT to walk through.

sam (sales-engineer): I'll join the call and prep a one-page note about token impact and idempotency keys.

ops-bot: Ticket created #POC-8342, linked traces req_deal_77/78, assigned to eng-runtime.

tyler (ml-eng): After the fix we'll add an integration test that opens a single connection with keep-alive, sends a request, forces a client retry immediately, and asserts responses are parseable independently. Also add a test to ensure function-calling arguments are not double-serialized.

ashley (acct-exec): Thanks team  I'll forward raw request when the customer sends it. :thumbsup:
